#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Live PCoin facts for the answer bot, read-only by construction.

WHY THIS EXISTS. Every number in the answer bot's prompt is a setting that lives
somewhere else, and the prompt goes stale silently. It has already published
wrong facts in the group -- a referral of 200 PCN when the live setting was 500,
a $100 daily withdrawal cap when the live cap was $50, and a denial that the pool
bounty existed 51 minutes after that bounty was announced. A prompt cannot be the
source of truth for a number an admin can change from a web page.

So the bot no longer remembers these figures. It reads them, here, at answer time.

READ-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY PROMISE. This tool performs HTTP GET against
public, unauthenticated endpoints and nothing else. It holds no credential, no
API key and no SSH identity, so there is no privilege here to misuse: it cannot
write, delete, edit or approve anything, even if something asked it to. That is a
deliberate choice over the alternative -- handing the model a credentialed tool
and trusting it to only read -- because read-only enforced by a request method is
a mechanism, and read-only enforced by an instruction is a wish. This estate has
already watched a survey agent run a write against a live database it had been
asked to only read.

A SOURCE THAT CANNOT BE READ SAYS SO. Every failure is reported as UNREADABLE and
never as absence, zero, or a remembered value. This is the estate's oldest rule
(CLAUDE.md 7.1, 7.2, and 8c rule 3): an unanswerable question must not collapse
into a confident answer. A bot that says "I cannot read the current fee right
now" is useful; one that invents a fee is the reason the bot was switched off.

NOTHING PER-USER IS EXPOSED HERE, AND NOTHING SHOULD BE ADDED. Every field below
is something we already publish on a public web page. Balances, deposits,
addresses, e-mail addresses and withdrawal rows are deliberately absent: the
answer bot posts into a PUBLIC group, so a tool that could read one person's
balance is one careless question away from publishing it.

    pcoin-facts            the block that goes into the prompt
    pcoin-facts --json     the same data, machine readable
    pcoin-facts --refresh  ignore the cache

Cache: /var/lib/pcoin-facts/cache.json, at most CACHE_SECONDS old, so a busy
minute in the group cannot become a burst against price.pc.am.
"""
import json
import os
import sys
import time
import re
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

CACHE = "/var/lib/pcoin-facts/cache.json"
CACHE_SECONDS = 60
TIMEOUT = 12

